      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Hypoxia has been implicated as a possible cause of adipose tissue inflammation. Furthermore, the acute phase protein serum amyloid A (SAA) has been associated with the modulation of the adipogenic process, and it is well-known that obese individuals have increased levels of SAA. The effect of hypoxia in the expression and production of SAA was examined in murine 3T3-L1 adipocytes. Hypoxia leads to a substantial increase in SAA3 mRNA and protein level, apparently in a time-dependent manner (threefold in 48 h), in fully differentiated 3T3-L1, followed by reestablishment of gene expression to basal levels after 24 h of reoxygenation. Hypoxia-induced SAA may be one of the key molecules to the development of the inflammatory response in adipose tissue."},
